WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.129
Okay, now let's talk about server and client setup, and in this first section, we will

2
00:00:06.129 --> 00:00:09.130
discuss working with Perforce Client.

3
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:13.009
To get started, you will need to head over to the Perforce website and locate the Helix

4
00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:19.559
P4V visual client and the Helix Core P4D server.

5
00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:23.559
Your initial setup will only need a single server, and while your team members or users

6
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:27.699
will install the client, your IT department should pretty much handle the rest of the

7
00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:32.719
hardware and server setup and configuration.

8
00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:38.320
Helix Core does offer a free trial for up to 5 users and up to 20 workspaces.

9
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.520
So if you need to make your own Perforce server to try it out first, go ahead and

10
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.009
give that a shot.

11
00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:49.189
Large, scalable hardware is not a requirement before you create your Perforce server.

12
00:00:49.189 --> 00:00:54.939
Now if you do select the full version, here are some of the Perforce Helix selling points.

13
00:00:54.939 --> 00:00:59.500
Full version Perforce Helix manages tens of thousands of developers and creatives, tens

14
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.500
of millions of daily transactions, hundreds of terabytes of data, and tens of thousands

15
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.659
of concurrent commits.

16
00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:13.709
Once you're ready to go, download the installer and install the Perforce client and server.

17
00:01:13.709 --> 00:01:15.590
Note here the server port.

18
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:21.709
It is highly recommended that you maintain your server port ID as the default, 1666.

19
00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:26.030
Additionally, the command line is going to need to be installed on the server in

20
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:32.409
order to utilize Unreal Game Sync in place of the standard Perforce front-end user interface.

21
00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.890
We'll discuss more about using Unreal Game Sync a little bit later in this course.

22
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.629
For now, I want to move on to discuss a little bit about hardware considerations

23
00:01:40.629 --> 00:01:47.709
you should make when choosing where you store your user workspace or using your server.

24
00:01:47.709 --> 00:01:51.450
So for server-side hardware, it's recommended that you consider using a Linux system

25
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.930
instead of Windows.

26
00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:58.530
A Linux system is going to give you a significantly faster performance over a Windows-based system,

27
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.329
but it's not a requirement.

28
00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:02.849
Most of what we'll talk about is going to be Windows-based.

29
00:02:02.849 --> 00:02:07.170
Additionally, I would also make sure that on both server and client system hardware

30
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.099
configurations that storage systems be deeply considered.

31
00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:17.280
The differences between mechanical hard drives and solid-state NVMe drives are significant.

32
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.060
Mechanical hard drives cannot read and write data concurrently, while solid-state NVMe drives

33
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.120
can read and write data concurrently.

34
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.039
For this reason, it is recommended that your server storage drive and client user workspace

35
00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:37.759
drive are SSD NVMe hardware.

36
00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:43.939
Okay, once Perforce Helix has been installed and the server depot is actively running,

37
00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:48.689
the next step is to open up the connection and create the client-side user workspace.

38
00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:54.289
The workspace is where your project files will live and where you will work on assets locally.

39
00:02:54.289 --> 00:02:59.449
This is where everything is going to be pushed and pulled to the Perforce server depot.

40
00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:06.780
I recommend naming the workspace something short, not the default Perforce-generated workspace name.

41
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.479
You should be aware of the OS limitations for characters and naming conventions that

42
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:12.240
you might run up against.

43
00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:19.030
For example, Windows has a 255 character limit, so simplicity in naming is key.

44
00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.330
Ultimately, your naming conventions and workspace should make sense for whatever project you're

45
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:28.949
working on, and it is expected that you're going to have multiple projects that you

46
00:03:28.949 --> 00:03:31.569
and your team will be working on.

47
00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:36.430
Most studios will have multiple project streams on their server and multiple user workspaces

48
00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.400
for each of those streams.

49
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.960
So once you're done, you want to select the depot, select the stream that you're

50
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.020
actually going to work from, and then you should be good to go.

51
00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.580
However, as you work, you may need to set up and use type maps.

52
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.699
Type maps are server-side information, and while the average user may not ever have to

53
00:03:56.699 --> 00:04:01.030
set this up, the type map is going to be used for any number of automated functions

54
00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.289
such as version trimming if your storage space is limited, or simply enumerating tasks

55
00:04:06.289 --> 00:04:07.650
as another example.

56
00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.949
To access the type map in Windows, just head over to the Windows PowerShell, type

57
00:04:12.949 --> 00:04:18.110
in the p4-type-map command, and that should open a text doc that you'll be able to

58
00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:23.470
customize, make edits, and save, implementing your automation without much fuss.

59
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:31.399
A good command-line type map example has been provided online in the Epic Games documentation.

60
00:04:31.399 --> 00:04:35.439
There you will find a comprehensive type map, similar to what is pictured here in

61
00:04:35.439 --> 00:04:38.199
this slide.

62
00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:41.620
Alright, moving on to the Helix Client frontend.

63
00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.639
This is your user interface, and I've broken this up into four distinct sections.

64
00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:47.600
First, there is the toolbar.

65
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.529
It's number one.

66
00:04:49.529 --> 00:04:52.129
This is where all your menu buttons are going to live.

67
00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:58.399
Refresh, get latest, submit, checkout, delete, differentiate, redraft, and clone.

68
00:04:58.399 --> 00:04:59.779
They're all there.

69
00:04:59.779 --> 00:05:04.240
And if you're ever unsure of what each toolbar button does once you launch Perforce, just

70
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.720
hover over the button and Perforce will give you a short description of the button's

71
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.389
function.

72
00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:14.819
The next window that I want to look at is number two, file versions.

73
00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:19.439
This is where your depot and workspace version views are available, and you can switch between

74
00:05:19.439 --> 00:05:23.329
them by clicking on the tabs for either section.

75
00:05:23.329 --> 00:05:28.089
Inside those sections, you'll be able to determine what files exist where, and be able to quickly

76
00:05:28.089 --> 00:05:33.370
check what is in your workspace and what is in your depot to compare versioning.

77
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.800
Typically, you're going to start out in the workspace when launching Perforce.

78
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.250
But in this particular image, I'm showing you the depot side.

79
00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.930
Next up is number three, the work area.

80
00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.350
The work area is where your files live.

81
00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.829
You can see pending change lists, there's a tab for the workspace setup, which will

82
00:05:53.829 --> 00:05:57.689
allow you to switch between multiple active workspaces.

83
00:05:57.689 --> 00:05:59.790
Notice here that I have two showing.

84
00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:07.629
One is for the unified content example version 5.2, and another one is for the version 5.3

85
00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:09.029
of the same content.

86
00:06:09.029 --> 00:06:14.189
Here, you can also switch over to view the history of anything that you have submitted

87
00:06:14.189 --> 00:06:16.660
for work.

88
00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:19.959
Below that is number four, the command line.

89
00:06:19.959 --> 00:06:24.779
This is an activity log, but it also allows for anything chronicled in the command line

90
00:06:24.779 --> 00:06:31.220
history to be copied and pasted for rapid scripting if needed.

91
00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.620
Moving on to the meat of Perforce, adding project files to Perforce that you can actually

92
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:41.670
work on, we're going to talk about adding an Unreal Engine project file to Perforce.

93
00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:46.230
Unreal Engine project files and folders will need to be added to the Perforce workspace

94
00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:51.430
once you've created that workspace on the client-side local hard drive.

95
00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:57.050
This will allow the files within that workspace to be accessible by Perforce.

96
00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.790
Once the workspace has been created on the client's local drive, all you need to do

97
00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:06.810
to add your project files is just copy the entire engine project folder from your

98
00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:13.850
Unreal Engine projects folder to the workspace collaboration subfolder, as you can see here.

99
00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:19.129
Just a quick note here, the hierarchy of the depot will be handled by your internal IT staff,

100
00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:25.430
but a typical configuration and what we use at Epic Games starts with a specific Perforce stream.

101
00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:31.350
Then under the Perforce stream, a collaboration folder is created, followed by a subfolder

102
00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:36.069
or set of subfolders that allows users to organize and maintain different sections

103
00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:38.389
of different projects.

104
00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:43.389
For example, right now, the ILT Education Department has the Unified Content Example

105
00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:50.670
folder where the ILT-UCE project lives, and that lives in both the workspace and

106
00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.110
the depot.

107
00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:58.149
If I want to add the ILT demo folder from my workspace to the project depot, I simply

108
00:07:58.149 --> 00:08:01.850
need to drag it over to the same workspace location.

109
00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.629
Now you might be wondering, what is that going to look like in Perforce once those

110
00:08:05.629 --> 00:08:10.230
project folders and files have been added to the client local workspace?

111
00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:16.220
Well, for Perforce to have access to the folder and files, all I need to do is refresh

112
00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:21.339
that workspace inside the Perforce workspace window, and that's going to show the folders

113
00:08:21.339 --> 00:08:25.160
and files that have been moved to that workspace.

114
00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.759
Typically the refresh happens automatically, so you don't have to actively refresh unless

115
00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:31.560
you don't see anything.

116
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.720
However, that doesn't mean that those folders and files have been pushed to the Perforce

117
00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.960
depot just yet.

118
00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.899
In order to get those folders and files up onto the depot so that they can be shared

119
00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:48.480
and accessed by multiple users, I need to select only a few specific folders and files

120
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.179
from inside the Perforce workspace file version window.

121
00:08:53.179 --> 00:08:58.879
Those specific folders are the Unreal Engine config folder, the content folder, and the

122
00:08:58.879 --> 00:09:03.799
Unreal Engine uProject file for my project.

123
00:09:03.799 --> 00:09:08.419
Once you have those folders and files selected, right-click on one of them and that will

124
00:09:08.419 --> 00:09:10.740
bring up a pop-up menu.

125
00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:15.240
This menu will have the option to mark your files for add.

126
00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.039
When you mark the files for add, another menu is going to pop up, allowing you to

127
00:09:19.039 --> 00:09:22.360
create a pending changelist.

128
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.639
When you first start out, that changelist that you're going to create is going to

129
00:09:25.639 --> 00:09:30.230
be a new one, and you can select that from the drop-down menu.

130
00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:35.750
You'll note here in the changelist description, I've added hashtag preflight to the start

131
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.470
of my description list.

132
00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.230
That is because there may be a project or stream lockdown in effect for different

133
00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.970
projects within Epic Games, and there may be changes to things going on behind the

134
00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.529
scenes that prevent a standard upload.

135
00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:54.549
You won't necessarily need to use this in your preflight or pending changelist.

136
00:09:54.549 --> 00:09:57.669
When you first start out, that changelist that you're going to create is going to be

137
00:09:57.669 --> 00:10:02.590
a new one, and you can select that from the drop-down menu as an option.

138
00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:07.590
You'll note here in the changelist description that I've added a hashtag preflight to the

139
00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.200
start of my description text.

140
00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:15.000
That is because there may be a project or stream lockdown in effect for different projects

141
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.019
within Epic Games, and there may be changes to things going on behind the scenes that

142
00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:22.559
may prevent a standard upload.

143
00:10:22.559 --> 00:10:26.879
It's not always necessary to add this to your description.

144
00:10:26.879 --> 00:10:32.940
Adding the specific hashtag preflight tag should allow me to bypass any lockdown blockades

145
00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:38.240
so that my submission continues to go through without disturbing other streams.

146
00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.779
After that tag, I give a normal description of what I'm actually submitting, what I'm

147
00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:48.039
doing to make my change, and also notating that I am the user making this change.

148
00:10:48.039 --> 00:10:50.740
Basically, I'm putting my name on it.

149
00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:55.980
Once I'm ready, then I just go ahead and click OK to create my pending changelist.

150
00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:01.320
From there, I might notice that hey, I forgot to add my source folder to that changelist.

151
00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:05.659
Source files like Maya, ZBrush, or any FBX file that I want to keep outside of the

152
00:11:05.659 --> 00:11:10.539
Unreal Engine project so that I might revise that again later can be added to the same

153
00:11:10.539 --> 00:11:15.220
pending changelist, or if I've already submitted that changelist, I can just create

154
00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.090
a new one for that submission.

155
00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.370
Process works exactly the same way.

156
00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:26.009
I just select and right-click those files or folder that I want to mark for add, and

157
00:11:26.009 --> 00:11:30.610
then if I still have an existing changelist that I haven't submitted yet, I can choose

158
00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:34.590
that changelist by selecting it from the pull-down menu that appears on the same

159
00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:35.590
pop-up menu.

160
00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:39.659
I don't have to change anything in the description because everything that I'm adding

161
00:11:39.659 --> 00:11:44.240
is already there, and then just go ahead and hit OK.

162
00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.440
What that's going to do is create a pending changelist file inside the work area window

163
00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.519
in Perforce.

164
00:11:50.519 --> 00:11:55.419
That is indicated by a red changelist triangle showing the active changelist waiting to be

165
00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:59.340
submitted and uploaded to the depot.

166
00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:03.700
To make that submission, just right-click on that red pending changelist and choose

167
00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.039
Submit.

168
00:12:06.039 --> 00:12:11.279
One thing to note, sometimes if you select only the folder and mark for add, the files

169
00:12:11.279 --> 00:12:14.940
within that folder may not be marked.

170
00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:19.360
If that happens, you will need to go in and select each file individually and then

171
00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:23.490
mark for add your entire selection.

172
00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:28.169
Once you choose to submit, you'll get another pop-up menu that will allow you to review

173
00:12:28.169 --> 00:12:32.159
all the files that are being submitted through the pending changelist.

174
00:12:32.159 --> 00:12:37.080
If everything looks good, just hit the Submit button, and then the Submit changelist progress

175
00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:39.090
bar will appear.

176
00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:42.730
From there, you want to verify that the files you've uploaded are now in the depot.

177
00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:47.549
So you're going to switch from your workspace view to the depot view inside the

178
00:12:47.549 --> 00:12:50.139
Perforce version window.

179
00:12:50.139 --> 00:12:54.019
You'll also want to verify that the log shows the submission of all the files that you had

180
00:12:54.019 --> 00:12:55.620
marked for add.

181
00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:00.899
You'll note here in the image for the log that shows the 347 files that were first

182
00:13:00.899 --> 00:13:06.179
added and then also lists the two additional files that were added later.

183
00:13:06.179 --> 00:13:11.720
When I submitted for the new changelist, 349 total files were added to the depot,

184
00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.759
verifying that all my files were added to the depot successfully.

185
00:13:15.759 --> 00:13:20.200
I can verify this visually by seeing that the project folder and files are now located

186
00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:25.980
under the ILT demo folder inside the ILT EDU depot folder.

187
00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:30.460
All those project files are now marked with a green indicator dot showing that they

188
00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:35.399
are active and ready to be checked out by other users that might need to work on them.

189
00:13:35.399 --> 00:13:37.700
Alright, that's it for this section.

190
00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:42.399
In our next section, we will continue discussing server-client setup and working with ShotGrid.
